 into  the very important role of the clinical  lab in ensuring safe practices  specimen integrity end-user protection  as well as ways  our customers are using daily to  maximize their own safety and efficiency  smell right now  thank you ryan hi again my name is  jordan henderson  i'm the asp certified clinical  laboratory scientist  and product manager here at the lab  conco corporation  and obviously selecting clinical lab  equipment is very  important there are a number of  questions that need to be  answered when going through this process  the first question to be answered should  be what type of protection  are we looking to achieve do we need  personnel and product protection  do we just need product protection or do  we not need  either personnel or product protection  the next question to be asked is what  enclosure are we looking to work in  are they class 2 or class 1 bses  are they vertical clean benches or piece  or hepa filter pcr enclosures  or are they non-ventilated enclosures  next determining  the application that will be performed  in these enclosures is important  these workflows generally are very  specific to the apartments where the  these enclosures will be departments  such as microbiology  pathology histology molecular diagnostic  or are we collecting and preparing  samples or reagents  obviously answering those three  questions you'll be pushed towards a  certain product type  and then of course evaluating the need  for any additional accessories is  important as well  a prime example of this is storage of  potentially hazardous chemicals  in chemical cabinets  and the reason we have to go through  this process is because clinical  diagnostic  work inherently increases the potential  to expose  clinical lab workers to aerosolize  infectious or biohazardous material  so really having a better understanding  of how this equipment  protects you and provides a level of  safety is very important  to you maintaining a high quality of  of work and diagnostic excellence  so really the question at hand is a hood  is a hood right  when i first started off in my clinical  lab  experience i would have said yes  knowing what i know now that is the  short answer to that question is  no um here on the screen there are a  variety of hoods  um that all serve very different  functions and that have different  requirements and are  governed by different regulatory bodies  first i would like to take a step back  and answer the question of what is  biosafety  and its impact on the clinical lab  setting  the cdc has outlined what biosafety is  in their bmbl  they have established four different  levels  of biosafety each level of biosafety is  determined by the agent that is being  used  the activity level that's being  performed on that agent  this will dictate the the equipment  requirements  depending on what you're handling and  what activities you're performing  and then there is also a facility design  and construction component that goes  hand in hand with the specific bio  safety levels  so what do these specific levels look  like  bio level one can consist of work that  can be performed in an  open bench  generally you're handling non-pathogenic  organisms such as yeast  and non-infectious strands of e coli  such as the k-12 strand  really the only construction requirement  with the bsl level one  is that a sink is available for hand  washing  moving on to bsl level two this level  meets all  bsl level 1 requirements  but incorporates either a class 1 or  class 2  biological safety enclosure depending on  if sterility is needed or not  additionally personal protective  equipment ppe is required  to be in use in a bsl-2 level laboratory  oftentimes these bfl level 2  laboratories  will have access to an autoclave or  another decontamination  procedure  moving on to bsl level 3  again bsl level 3 meets all the  requirements of bsl level 2  however due to the agents that are being  handled  in these laboratory environments a  buffered corridor  must exist in these laboratories and  essentially this is just a shower in  shower out space  for laboratory workers to to  wash off and again further reduce  the exposure to aerosolized infectious  agents  this laboratory will have self-closing  doors  and the exhaust needs to work under  negative pressure  the most severe level of biosafety is  bsl level  4 and again it meets all vsl3 level  requirements  and oftentimes either either highly  restricted or entirely separate  buildings with their own dedicated  plumbing  and other utility lines  the type of dsc that can be used in this  in conjunction with positive pressure  suits as indicated in the image to the  left  or a class 1 or class 2 bsc  or a class 3 bfc  if they're not using positive pressure  pressure suits  an example of a class 3 bfc  is going to be a laminar flow glove box  that is a totally enclosed  and is leak tight  so i'd like to talk about bio safety  cabinets  my bio safety cabinets essentially  exists to protect laboratory workers  such as  medical lab scientists as yourself and  their surrounding environments  from exposure to aerosolized infectious  material  by incorporating filters in these  cabinets  that is how these these enclosures help  track those particulates and aerosols  by doing so this allows for more complex  procedures to be  done more safely and efficiently  so to understand a little bit more of  how these  biosafety cabinets operate we need to  understand what laminar or  unidirectional airflow is  so really what laminar airflow is  is air moving in a uniform direction at  a uniform velocity  why is this important is because that  uniform direction of airflow  under that uniform velocity helps sweep  biohazardous material away which  controls contamination  and by manipulating those airflow  patterns just a little bit more we're  able to provide sterile environments to  work in  on this slide here is just a  representation of the air flow patterns  in a class 2 a2 bfc  inward air is pulled through the sash  opening towards the rear baffle  where the blower then redirects it  towards the top of the unit  that inward airflow is offering that  personnel protection  once the air is pushed through a hepa  filter  in a downward fashion that air is then  used to provide product protection that  we're looking for  exhausted air is also pushed through a  hepa filter  which provides clean air to the to the  environment that that lab tech is  working in  so to go a little bit deeper in bio  safety cabinets  they are broken into classes there are  three classes class one class  two and class three for the most part  class two bscs are going to be commonly  found in the clinical laboratory  they are further subdivided into types  and the most common types that we will  find in a clinical lab are going to be  your a1s and your a2s  this bsc offers personnel product  and environmental protection for  microbiol  microbiological workflows and processes  again as i had mentioned previously the  inward air flow is responsible for  protecting the end user or the medical  lab professional  the downward hepa filtered laminar  airflow  is responsible for protecting your  samples and products  from contamination and then the hepa  filtered exhaust air  is going to be recirculated  back into the environment  when it comes to contaminated ductworks  and plenums  just please note that these need to be  operated under a negative pressure to  further reduce  exposure risk risk of aerosolized  infectious material  here's another representation of the  anticipated airflow  in a type type 2 a2 bsc  the picture on the right is just showing  again  airflow being sucked in through the sash  opening being redirected  through the back plenum uh via the  blower  and forced either through two filters  one to exhaust or one to provide that  downward airflow  for product protection the image on the  left  is indicating really  the work area and work surface due to  these type 2 or i'm sorry class 2 type  a2  bscs recirculating air it's not  recommended that  um chemicals are are used  just so after recirculating  hazardous chemicals um they could  concentrate and cause  problems so just be aware of that  um you can't talk bs without talking  hepa filters and hepa filters are high  efficiency particulate air filters  that consist of four components the  filter media the frame  the bonding agent and the gasket these  filters are 99.9  effective at filtering out particulates  at a  0.3 micron size  this allows us to provide iso class 5 or  better  air and of course as i mentioned before  all bses are com come with two  two hepa filters one to filter the  supply air  one to filter the exhaust there  when it comes to bio safety resources as  i mentioned the cdc  and the cdc's bl bmbl  fifth edition is a great resource in  understanding what biosafety is and how  to handle potentially infectious agents  i provided a link here for reference in  the future  or if you're just interested in what the  cdc thinks about this  when it comes to the design requirements  of class 2 bses  the nsf standard 49 is going to be  a great resource to look at in  understanding  the design requirements for biological  safety cabinets  additional bio safety resources  the controlled environment testing  association or ceta  is going to be responsible for the  exhaust system requirements for class  2bses  there is a fee associated with this one  i've provided the link below  other important resources to be aware of  are the world health organization which  is free  absa uh in it that  nih uh which is also free and then of  course  the canadian bioscience i'm sorry bio  safety standards and guidelines as well  so where are these products found in the  laboratory  my first experience when i started my  clinical lab journey  was in microbiology biological safety  cabinets  serves as an ideal workstation for the  numerous  microbiology workflows that are done  every day  these workflows include setting up test  cli test kits  such as flu ab rsv rapid testing  fecal occults or other molecular test  kits like  rapid flu a b testing  setting up and drying grand stained  slides prior to staining  and then this also gives us a workspace  to streak microbiology plates  for later identification and  susceptibility testing as well  another solution that is commonly found  throughout the lab  is going to be a filtered pcr enclosure  essential essentially this solution is  just a vertical clean bench  that protects your work area the reason  why this is important  is because obviously when you're doing  pcr testing or other nucleic acid  amplification testing  really limiting the contamination risk  is important because as you know  when contaminants are amplified it just  throws your entire runs off  however it should be noted that  the type of airflow that's provided only  provides  product protection it does not protect  the end user  so great care needs to be taken when  evaluating the use of these  solutions if you are using biohazardous  material  various toxins or other radionucleotides  potentially  we don't recommend this solution at all  so again careful assessment is required  to make sure everyone is being  being considered and things are done  safely and effectively  oftentimes these solutions will come  with uv lights incorporated in with them  obviously when we're dealing with rna  and dna  the presence of them poses a risk of  contamination so  incorporating uv light actually  decontaminates  um residual dna that's left over  to again provide a level  of safety and efficiency when using  these products  so where do we find these pcr enclosures  in the lab  well obviously the molecular department  is going to be an ideal situation for  for these products again  for workflows pcr or  nucleic acid the amplification testing  that  still incorporate a pretty manual micro  micro  well um plating procedure this is going  to be a place where that's going to be  carried out  moving on to another solution that's  commonly found within the laboratory  environment are going to be your  duckless carbon filtered enclosures  oftentimes  these are just referred to as ductless  hoods  and the reason why is because they serve  as viable alternatives to their  traditional ducted hoods  these solutions are going to give you  the ability to work with volatile  chemicals and vapors  they can handle nuisance odonts and  non-toxic chemicals  and then they can also be used in  non-toxic  to handle non-toxic particulates as well  really the regulatory body that's  responsible for the divine  defining this is going to be fifa and  we are guided by fifa 9-2  the fifa 9-2010 ductless hood  classification is going to be  responsible for three classifications of  ductless hoods the first classification  is dh1 this is just stating that  ductless hoods should be designed  to filter non-toxic chemicals nuisance  odors and particulates  moving on to dh2 this level meets all  the dh1 requirements  but is equipped with the filtration able  to handle an approved  toxic contaminants up to a break  breakthrough point only moving on to dh  level  3 this meets all the dh level 2  requirements  and is equipped with the filtration  mechanism to handle approved toxic  contaminants  up to and beyond their breakthrough  point  via secondary filter  so why would a clinical lab want to  incorporate ductless hoods  into their clinical lab setting  obviously not being hard ducted to  existing ductwork provides a level of  flexibility that hospitals  routinely sync and require oftentimes  these  clinical laboratories are put in lower  levels of hospitals  or they're in older buildings that it's  just hard to  modify existing ductwork  again not being ducted to existing  ductwork  makes it for a very easy installation  process  and it is associated with reduced cost  by streamlining the installation process  obviously not being fixed means these  things are more portable  so they can move room to room to  accommodate  um whether departments move or workflows  need to be  done in a different environment  these are sustainable obviously ducted  hoods don't have  makeup air requirements and finally  ducted hoods uh  tend to be less green so an inducted uh  ductless hood  actually doesn't vent uh conditioned air  which is saving um  money as well one uh so where are  ductless hoods gonna be found in the  clinical laboratory  the first place that i see dr douglas  hoods  in the clinical laboratory is going to  be the pathology department  and often it's going to be during the  tissue grossing  segment of pathological workflows  obviously because  those specimens are transported  and preserve it as ductless hoods are  going to be an ideal  spot to manage those odors and chemicals  additionally you can find ductless hoods  in the cytology and histology  departments  during the tissue staying process um  those chemicals  can have strong odors and ductless are  ideal to manage that as well  but when it comes to cover slipping  ductless hoods  can be utilized here as well um because  of the phosphate  buffer formula and preservative for  storage of these slides  um ducted hoods are are are an ideal  solution to handle those odors  so we've discussed a number of solutions  a number of  regulations that that should be  considered  who's responsible for really leveraging  all this information  that's where the biosafety officer comes  in  the role of the biosafety officer is  important because they're directly  responsible for developing safety  policies and procedures they're going to  be responsible for training of  laboratory staff  to make sure that they're following  the established procedures to ensure  their safety as well  they'll also consult in advising on from  a safety perspective throughout the  laboratory  they also ensure compliance with the  safety policies and procedures  they will assist and perform regular  safety inspections to make sure  everything is up  up to par they will investigate and  document  laboratory accidents and report on those  accidents as well  in my experience as a clinical lab  professional  this role was consisted of  multiple people oftentimes clinical  coordinators or department supervisors  really understanding the workflows and  what is required of them  from a diagnostic perspective in  conjunction with  building managers and environmental  service managers  to make sure that the mechanical  mechanical components  are being considered as well so this  concludes the end of the presentation  and i'd like to say thank you  future of healthcare and patient safety  is paramount to our well-being  that is as is the ability to provide  accurate and timely test results  safely and affordably while also  minimizing risk and improving the lives  of patients around the world your role  in this equation is crucial  so today we thank you and recognize that  you are the hidden heroes behind  successful patient outcome so for more  solutions in regards to the clinical  laboratory environment  please visit labconco.com clinical  i would like to open the floor for any  questions  that any attendees may have at this  moment  thank you ryan and jordan for your  informative presentations  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar  if you have a question you'd like to ask  please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  we'll answer as many questions as we  have time for  okay let's go ahead and get started  let's see  our first question is what  features of lab equipment should i be  looking for  to maximize efficiency in the molecular  lab setting  um so that's a good question obviously  when it comes to the molecular testing  like i said a little bit ago  it's really limiting the the presence of  any potential contaminants because  obviously you know when you do  pcr testing or nucleic acid testing when  we start to amplify things  contaminants uh tend to get amplified as  well  and so when those contaminants are  present and they get amplified obviously  that can throw a wrench into the quality  of your results  additionally  having to redo testing is time consuming  energy consuming  and cost consuming so it's very  important to have an enclosure that can  really manage  uh to provide that that quality working  environment  and that's where those pcr enclosures  come  come from so obviously pulling in hepa  filter  downward flowing laminar air is going to  be responsible really  offering that product protection that  that you need when it comes to those  sensitive  molecular workflows  thank you jordan that's great all right  this next  question is what is the ideal  environment  for work with covid19 samples and why  uh so this is um you know a pretty  common question that  um that a lot of people have been asking  obviously this past year  um from a clinical lab perspective  um we need to consider the types of  specimens that can be contaminated so  you obviously have your  respiratory specimens like sputum your  whole blood your serum your plasma  or potentially even urine specimens  all of these should of course be handled  as withstand  in the assumption of standard  precautions at the  level 2 biosafety level  and specifically like some workflows  working with intact or full full length  genomic rna that's going to be done in a  bsl level 2 laboratory under those  those recommendations um also worth  mentioning that  any activities involving manipulation of  untreated specimens can also be  performed in a bsl level 2 facility  while using bsl  3 practices when it comes to  more of a research side so cell  propagation  um from culture i'm sorry propagation in  cell cultures  uh and even just the initial  identification or characterization  of um coping 19 samples  and their recovery actually should be  